%I A399526 #16 Sep 01 2026 10:36:36
%S A399526 1,29,1229,4069,49294073,6895437113
%N A399526 The least start of the longest possible run of successive prime(n)-rough numbers that are all squarefree.
%C A399526 A p-rough number is a positive integer that is not divisible by any prime less than p. For example, the 2-rough numbers are all the positive integers, and the 3-rough numbers are the odd positive integers.
%C A399526 The length of runs of consecutive squarefree prime(n)-rough numbers is bounded, as shown by the following argument.
%C A399526 The sequence of prime(n)-rough numbers is periodic modulo the (n-1)-th primorial, A002110(n-1). The terms in each period are the totatives of A002110(n-1) (i.e., the A005867(n-1) terms forming the (n-1)-th row of A286941). The maximum gap between consecutive prime(n)-rough numbers is A048670(n-1) = A048669(A002110(n-1)).
%C A399526 Consider the subsequence of the prime(n)-rough numbers formed by multiplying each prime(n)-rough number by prime(n)^2. These numbers are themselves prime(n)-rough, but because they are multiples of prime(n)^2, they are not squarefree. The maximum difference between consecutive terms in this nonsquarefree subsequence is A048670(n-1) * prime(n)^2. Consequently, the maximum size of any run of consecutive squarefree prime(n)-rough numbers is bounded by this maximum gap.
%C A399526 If there exist two consecutive prime(n)-rough numbers, r_1 and r_2, such that all prime(n)-rough numbers strictly between r_1 * prime(n)^2 and r_2 * prime(n)^2 are squarefree, then a(n) is equal to the least prime(n)-rough number in this range. If no such pair exists, a(n) is still well-defined, but its value must be found and proven using a different method. The values of a(n) for n <= 6 were found using this method.
%C A399526 The longest possible length of a run of squarefree prime(7)-rough numbers is at most 1218. If the longest run length is 1218, then a(7) > 5 * 10^13. Otherwise, a(7) >= 74760200089, where this lower bound corresponds to a run of length 768.
%C A399526 The corresponding longest possible lengths are listed in A399527.
%H A399526 Amiram Eldar, <a href="/A399526/a399526.txt">Mathematica code for A399526 and A399527</a>.
%H A399526 Amiram Eldar, <a href="/A399526/a399526_1.txt">PARI code for A399526 and A399527</a>.
%H A399526 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/RoughNumber.html">Rough Number</a>.
%H A399526 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rough_number">Rough number</a>.
%e A399526 a(1) = 1, as 1 is the smallest starting term of a maximal run of 3 consecutive integers ("prime(1)-rough numbers") that are all squarefree: 1, 2, and 3. The starting terms of such runs are listed in A007675. There are no runs of length exceeding 3, because every sequence of 4 consecutive integers contains a multiple of 4 = 2^2.
%e A399526 a(2) = 29, as 29 is the smallest starting term of a maximal run of 8 consecutive odd integers ("prime(2)-rough numbers") that are all squarefree: 29, 31, 33, 35, 37, 39, 41, and 43. The starting terms of such runs are listed in A398951. There are no runs of length exceeding 8, because every sequence of 9 consecutive odd integers contains a multiple of 9 = 3^2.
